// xpute's link in WIT's shape; the execution meaning is xpute's (xpute-core
// link.rs). WIT's grammar is used as far as xpute needs it: interfaces, `func`
// and `async func`, resources and their methods, records, enums, variants
// whose cases carry one payload each, lists, options, bool, the integers to
// 32 bits, f32, f64 and strings.
//
// Whatever is called is an object. A URI names it: a tuple of tokens, each an
// s32 or a string. A singleton interface is named by its package and its
// name, ("xpute:core", "timer"), and a file by the program's path tokens. The
// side that serves an object issues its handle, and a frame's command carries
// a handle its callee issued, the method's number and the RES bit (31): the
// handle in bits 8..30, the method in bits 0..7. A method's number is its
// place in its interface, in declaration order: a singleton interface's
// functions, or one resource's methods.
//
// The root, handle 0, has one method, `open(uri) -> handle`, number 0, which
// the serving side answers at once from the opener mounted at the URI's
// longest matching leading tokens. An open is pipelined, so a missing file
// opens, and its first async method answers ENOENT. Every handle has a
// release, method 0xFF, which takes no result and lets the object go; a
// resource whose end must report a result keeps its own method for it, as a
// file's `close`. A program opens every singleton it calls once, at boot.
//
// A core has three exports. `link_init` lays the link and answers its layout,
// the inbox and a frame's room, and runs nothing of the program. `link_boot`
// is the core's first entry: the program's boot opens what it calls, and only
// then does the entry start. A program opens the stop first, so a bug in its
// boot is reported. `link_in` enters the core with
// the frame laid in its inbox. The runtime calls init, reads the layout, then
// boot, so every entry comes from a runtime that knows the room. A trap out
// of any export stops the core for good.
//
// A method that may answer later is an `async func`, and is sent; any other
// always answers at once. An `async func` always answers later, with
// EINPROGRESS, so waiting on one leaves the core's entry. The core runs only
// when the runtime enters it: with a request, an answer, or the answer of
// what it armed, such as `timer.sleep`.
//
// Raw memory enters WIT in one place, `link.lent`: a span of the caller's
// memory, (at, len), the callee's to read or write until the answer. The
// caller's binding takes a buffer it owns, lends it with the call and hands
// it back with the answer, so the lend lasts as long as the ownership; a call
// dropped before its answer keeps the buffer alive until the answer comes.
//
// A bool, an unsigned integer or a handle returns in the answer's result,
// below 2^31; any other return, in its packet. A variant crosses as its
// payload alone, whose node's type tells its case, so no two cases cross as
// one type; an option's none is a nil node.
//
// The link's crossings (xpute-core link.rs) are no world's: a world holds
// only interfaces, and a program's world only those it uses.

package xpute:core@0.1.0;

// What the link itself takes.
interface link {
  // A URI's token.
  variant token {
    int(s32),
    str(string),
  }

  // A span of the caller's memory, lent until the answer.
  record lent {
    at: u32,
    len: u32,
  }
}
